    <subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-elliptic_integral_of_first_kind">
      <title>Elliptic Integral of the First Kind and The Period of the Nonlinear Pendulum</title>
      <p>
        In this subsection, we will discuss how to approximate the value of the elliptic integral 
        of the first kind using Taylor series, which is defined as follows:
      </p>
      <me>
        K(z) = \int_{0}^{\pi/2} \frac{d\theta}{\sqrt{1 - z^2 \sin^2 \theta}},
      </me>
      <p>
      where  <m>|z| \lt 1</m></p>.
      <p>
        We will use the Taylor series for <m>f(x) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}</m> to approximate the 
        value of <m>K(z)</m> as follows:
      </p>
      <me>
        K(z) = \int_{0}^{\pi/2} \frac{d\theta}{\sqrt{1 - z^2 \sin^2 \theta}} 
        = \int_{0}^{\pi/2} f(z \sin \theta) d\theta
      </me>
      <p>
        We can use the Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m> 
        to approximate <m>K(z)</m>. The Taylor series for <m>f(x)</m> at <m>x=0</m> is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        f(x) = 1 + \frac{1}{2}x^2 + \frac{3}{8}x^4 + \frac{5}{16}x^6 + \cdots
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, we have:
      </p>
      <md>
       <mrow>  K(z) \amp = \int_{0}^{\pi/2} f(z \sin \theta) d\theta </mrow>
       <mrow> \amp       = \int_{0}^{\pi/2} \left( 1 + \frac{1}{2}z^2 \sin^2 \theta 
        + \frac{3}{8}z^4 \sin^4 \theta + \frac{5}{16}z^6 \sin^6 \theta + \cdots \right) d\theta </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        We can integrate term by term to get:
      </p>
      <me>
        K(z) = \frac{\pi}{2} + \frac{\pi}{8} z^2 + \frac{9\pi}{128} z^4 + \frac{25\pi}{512} z^6 + \cdots.
      </me>
      <p>
        For comparison purposes, we will consider the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>z=0</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        In figure <xref ref="fig-prefigure-elliptic-integral-K"/>, we show the graph of <m>K(z)</m> along with 
        two Taylor approximations of the zeroth order, <m>p_0(z)=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, and the second order, 
        <m>p_2(z)=\frac{\pi}{2}+\frac{\pi}{8}z^2</m>, centered at <m>z=0</m>. As expected and can be observed from the graph, 
        the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 provides a better approximation to <m>K(z)</m> than the Taylor polynomial of degree 0.  
        Note that as value of <m>z</m> increases, the zeroth order approximation becomes less accurate very quickly, while the second order approximation  
        remains relatively accurate for a larger range of <m>z</m> values. This is consistent with our expectation that the fact that 
        the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 should provide a better approximation to <m>K(z)</m> than the Taylor polynomial of degree 0.
      </p>
    
