      <example>
        <title>Approximating the period of the nonlinear pendulum using Taylor series</title>

        <p>
        The exact expression for the period of a pendulum of length <m>l</m> is given by:
        </p>
        <me>
          T = 4 \sqrt{\frac{l}{g}} K(\sin(\theta_0/2)),
        </me>
        <p>
        where <m>g</m> is the acceleration due to gravity and <m>\theta_0</m> is the 
        maximum angular displacement of the pendulum. 
        </p>
        <p>
        In most introductory courses, the period of a pendulum of length <m>l</m> for small angles is 
        approximated by using the constant term in the Taylor series for <m>K(z)</m> to get:
        </p>
        <me>
          T = 2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{l}{g}}.
        </me>
        <p>
        Find the period of a pendulum of length <m>l</m> for a maximum angular displacement 
        of <m>\theta_0</m> using the Taylor series for <m>K(z)</m> up to the term in <m>z^2</m>.
        What is the error in this approximation? and compare it to the error in using the constant term in the Taylor series 
        for <m>K(z)</m> to approximate the period of the pendulum.
        </p>
